{
  "requirement": "Under {cite}`requirements.cli.skill_structure`, the `habeas` skill MUST be the home of routing tasks to skills, `habeas status` and `habeas info`, directive and reference syntax, distributed requirements, and gotchas common to every task.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject says the `habeas` skill MUST be the home of five topics: routing tasks to skills, `habeas status` and `habeas info`, directive and reference syntax, distributed requirements, and gotchas common to every task. The cited parent `requirements.cli.skill_structure` adds that a topic is stated in full in exactly one skill. I judged the test with the exemption-free criteria and found no gap.\n\nThe only marked test is `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_habeas_skill_is_home_of_routing_inspection_syntax_and_shared_gotchas]`. It covers each obligation as follows:\n\n- **Routing:** `section(raw, \"## Workflow\")` must contain the `| Task | Skill |` table header.\n- **`habeas status` and `habeas info`:** the `## Inspecting` section must contain both command forms.\n- **Directive syntax:** the `## Requirements, examples, and definitions` section must contain the `{req}`, `{example}` and `{definition}` descriptions.\n- **Reference syntax:** the `## References` section must contain the phrases about writing a reference in a comment on the source line and about fencing a reference.\n- **Gotchas common to every task:** the `## Gotchas` section must contain \"Reference discovery scans the working directory\".\n- **Distributed requirements:** `references/distributed-requirements.md` must exist in the habeas skill and have the heading `# Distributed requirements`. The existence check works because `skill_file` panics if the file is missing.\n- **Detailed syntax:** `references/syntax-details.md` must contain the sections on nested-chapter full names and on anchors.\n\nThe `assert_text` helper really asserts, with `contains` on the collapsed section text. The `section` helper extracts the named section and ignores headings inside code fences.\n\nThe closing `assert_home(\"habeas\")` call covers the parent's \"each topic stated in full in exactly one skill\" clause for this skill. Through `assert_phrases` it requires every `home_markers(\"habeas\")` phrase to be in the habeas skill (`SKILL.md` plus its references). Those phrases include the routing table, the selection and glob syntax, nested-chapter full names, fenced references, `:::{violations}`, \"Each site-query match makes a site\", and the gotcha phrases. It also asserts that none of the other four skills in `SKILLS` contains any of those markers. A skill that moved or duplicated these topics would fail it.\n\nThe distributed-requirements check is the weakest part. Its direct assertion is only a heading, and the substantive distributed-requirements marker is checked at whole-skill level by `assert_home`. That is enough for a text-structure requirement of this kind, and I did not find a plausible violating state that the test would accept. I found no unacknowledged gaps and no marking gaps.",
  "references": "3e613787d62d50092286fd528020c2381ceb86235c44291883f55c4a6d9690e7",
